1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,744
After three days of rain, a man throwing dead crabs into the sea finds something groaning at the back of his courtyard: a very old man, face down in the mud, who cannot get up because of his enormous, dirty wings.

2
00:00:13,967 --> 00:00:15,088
Is he an angel?

3
00:00:15,088 --> 00:00:16,885
The village is not sure.

4
00:00:16,885 --> 00:00:21,223
So it does the most human thing possible: it charges five cents to look at him.

5
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:34,713
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 7: A Very Old Man with Enormous Wings, by Gabriel García Márquez.

6
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:38,195
Six parts.

7
00:00:38,195 --> 00:00:40,747
Márquez and magical realism.

8
00:00:40,747 --> 00:00:42,128
An angel in the mud.

9
00:00:42,128 --> 00:00:43,990
From wonder to sideshow.

10
00:00:43,990 --> 00:00:45,800
Decline and flight.

11
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,065
The characters and themes.

12
00:00:48,065 --> 00:00:49,405
And how the magic is told.

13
00:00:53,767 --> 00:01:04,263
Gabriel García Márquez, known as Gabo, was born in nineteen twenty seven in Aracataca, a small town in Colombia, and raised by his grandparents.

14
00:01:04,263 --> 00:01:12,623
His grandmother told stories of ghosts and omens in a flat, matter of fact voice, and that voice became his own.

15
00:01:12,623 --> 00:01:19,901
A journalist turned novelist, he wrote One Hundred Years of Solitude and won the Nobel Prize in nineteen eighty two.

16
00:01:19,901 --> 00:01:21,904
This story is from nineteen sixty eight.

17
00:01:23,133 --> 00:01:35,726
This is the classic example of magical realism: impossible events told in exactly the same calm tone as ordinary ones, so the magic feels like part of everyday life.

18
00:01:35,726 --> 00:01:42,106
The narrator never explains it, and the characters soon find it a nuisance rather than a marvel.

19
00:01:42,106 --> 00:01:47,500
An angel arrives, and he smells of the outdoors and is fed eggplant mush.

20
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:54,893
Pelayo fetches his wife Elisenda.

21
00:01:54,893 --> 00:02:03,500
The old man is nearly bald, nearly toothless, dressed like a ragpicker, his wings like a buzzard's, dirty and half plucked.

22
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:10,401
He answers them in an unknown dialect with a sailor's voice, so they sensibly decide he is a castaway.

23
00:02:10,401 --> 00:02:18,773
The neighbour woman, who knows everything about life and death, needs one look: he is an angel, come for their sick child.

24
00:02:18,773 --> 00:02:27,263
Father Gonzaga, the priest, greets him in Latin; he does not understand, and up close he seems much too human.

25
00:02:27,263 --> 00:02:30,296
He writes to his bishop, who will write to Rome.

26
00:02:30,296 --> 00:02:33,302
And the crowd dreams of making him mayor of the world.

27
00:02:34,533 --> 00:02:37,803
Pelayo locks him in the chicken coop with the hens.

28
00:02:37,803 --> 00:02:41,292
That night the rain stops and the baby's fever breaks.

29
00:02:41,292 --> 00:02:48,324
Feeling generous, the couple plan to put the angel on a raft with food and water and leave him to the sea.

30
00:02:48,324 --> 00:02:54,308
But at dawn the whole neighbourhood is at the wire, throwing in food as if he were a circus animal.

31
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:03,802
The courtyard becomes a marketplace, and soldiers have to break up the mob.

32
00:03:03,802 --> 00:03:11,862
Elisenda, aching from sweeping up the litter, has an idea: fence the yard and charge five cents to see the angel.

33
00:03:11,862 --> 00:03:22,096
Pilgrims come from far away: a woman who has counted her heartbeats since childhood and run out of numbers, a man kept awake by the noise of the stars.

34
00:03:22,096 --> 00:03:24,639
Within a week the house is crammed with money.

35
00:03:25,867 --> 00:03:28,694
The angel takes no part in his own show.

36
00:03:28,694 --> 00:03:32,496
His only supernatural virtue seems to be patience.

37
00:03:32,496 --> 00:03:39,632
Hens peck at him, invalids tear out his feathers, even kind people throw stones to make him stand.

38
00:03:39,632 --> 00:03:45,699
He refuses mothballs, which the neighbour says angels eat, and lives on eggplant mush.

39
00:03:45,699 --> 00:03:51,624
Once, when he lies so still they think he is dead, they burn his side with a branding iron.

40
00:03:51,624 --> 00:03:58,616
He wakes in tears, shouting in his unknown language, and beats his wings in a storm of dust.

41
00:03:58,616 --> 00:04:05,561
After that they are careful: his calm was not that of a hero at rest, but of a cataclysm in repose.

42
00:04:06,767 --> 00:04:13,279
Meanwhile the Church investigates whether he has a navel and whether his dialect is Aramaic.

43
00:04:13,279 --> 00:04:20,727
Then a providential event: a travelling show brings a woman who was changed into a spider for disobeying her parents.

44
00:04:20,727 --> 00:04:41,977
She is cheaper, you may ask her questions, and her sad story has so much human truth and so clear a moral that she easily defeats the aloof angel, whose miracles are faintly ridiculous: a blind man who grows three new teeth instead of sight, a leper whose sores sprout sunflowers.

45
00:04:41,977 --> 00:04:46,207
The crowd leaves, and the courtyard is as empty as when it rained.

46
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,981
The couple have no complaints.

47
00:04:52,981 --> 00:04:59,595
With the money they build a two storey mansion, with iron bars on the windows so that angels can't get in.

48
00:04:59,595 --> 00:05:05,129
Pelayo gives up his job and keeps rabbits; Elisenda buys satin shoes.

49
00:05:05,129 --> 00:05:11,106
The child grows up and plays in the rotting coop, and he and the angel catch chicken pox together.

50
00:05:11,106 --> 00:05:21,794
The doctor who listens to the angel's chest finds so many noises he seems impossible alive, but the wings look so natural that the doctor wonders why other men don't have them.

51
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:24,486
The coop collapses.

52
00:05:24,486 --> 00:05:36,947
The angel drags himself about the house like a stray dying man; Elisenda chases him out with a broom and cries that it is awful living in a hell full of angels.

53
00:05:36,947 --> 00:05:40,853
One winter he has a fever, and they fear he will die.

54
00:05:40,853 --> 00:05:46,752
But he survives, and with the spring sun, stiff new feathers grow, which he hides.

55
00:05:46,752 --> 00:05:52,754
One morning, while Elisenda is cutting onions, a wind from the sea blows in.

56
00:05:52,754 --> 00:05:59,890
She watches his clumsy first attempts, nails ploughing the vegetable patch, and then he gains height.

57
00:05:59,890 --> 00:06:08,291
She sighs with relief, for herself and for him, until he is only an imaginary dot on the horizon of the sea.

58
00:06:13,067 --> 00:06:14,293
The characters.

59
00:06:14,293 --> 00:06:20,738
The old man is never identified: angel, castaway, or a Norwegian with wings.

60
00:06:20,738 --> 00:06:29,813
He is human in every detail, smelly, sick and old, and endlessly patient, and in the end he leaves on his own terms.

61
00:06:29,813 --> 00:06:39,175
Pelayo and Elisenda are neither heroes nor villains: too kind to kill him, scared enough to cage him, quick to make money.

62
00:06:39,175 --> 00:06:43,238
Father Gonzaga is the slow, sceptical Church.

63
00:06:43,238 --> 00:06:47,555
And the crowd is curious, greedy, cruel, and fickle.

64
00:06:48,767 --> 00:06:49,680
Themes.

65
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:57,701
Wonder turned into spectacle: the main irony is that a miracle is met with entertainment and profit rather than awe.

66
00:06:57,701 --> 00:07:04,094
Greed: faith becomes business, and the profits build a house with bars against angels.

67
00:07:04,094 --> 00:07:06,555
Cruelty to the weak and old.

68
00:07:06,555 --> 00:07:14,263
And appearance and reality: this angel is filthy, sick and old, so what is an angel, really?

69
00:07:14,263 --> 00:07:18,974
Behind them all, the old man's final flight is the story's one moment of freedom.

70
00:07:23,767 --> 00:07:25,344
How is the magic told?

71
00:07:25,344 --> 00:07:30,370
Every impossible thing is described in the flattest everyday terms.

72
00:07:30,370 --> 00:07:33,313
A man with wings, and he has lice.

73
00:07:33,313 --> 00:07:37,258
A woman turned into a spider, and she is cheaper to see.

74
00:07:37,258 --> 00:07:40,852
Healing miracles, and they are consolation prizes.

75
00:07:40,852 --> 00:07:48,773
Because nobody in the story truly marvels, we see clearly what ordinary people do with a marvel, and that is the satire.

76
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:51,499
Devices to quote.

77
00:07:51,499 --> 00:07:53,582
Magical realism itself.

78
00:07:53,582 --> 00:07:58,595
Irony: a mansion with bars to keep angels out, built with angel money.

79
00:07:58,595 --> 00:08:03,048
Satire on authority: the Church asks whether he has a navel.

80
00:08:03,048 --> 00:08:05,418
Imagery of beauty in squalor.

81
00:08:05,418 --> 00:08:09,351
Simile: the patience of a dog who had no illusions.

82
00:08:09,351 --> 00:08:15,706
And an open ending: he becomes an imaginary dot on the horizon, and we never learn what he was.

83
00:08:18,133 --> 00:08:18,904
Pause and think.

84
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:32,489
The spider woman is cheaper, talks, and has "so much human truth": the fickle crowd moves on.

85
00:08:34,467 --> 00:08:34,902
One more.

86
00:08:42,533 --> 00:08:47,587
Impossible events are reported as matter-of-factly as ordinary ones.

87
00:08:49,767 --> 00:08:50,823
Quick recap.

88
00:08:50,823 --> 00:08:53,922
García Márquez and magical realism.

89
00:08:53,922 --> 00:08:56,006
A winged old man in the mud.

90
00:08:56,006 --> 00:09:00,511
A coop, five cents a look, and a spider woman who steals the show.

91
00:09:00,511 --> 00:09:04,248
A house barred against angels, and a final flight.

92
00:09:04,248 --> 00:09:05,923
Wonder turned into spectacle.

93
00:09:10,100 --> 00:09:13,214
That was A Very Old Man with Enormous Wings.

94
00:09:13,214 --> 00:09:17,667
Next we turn to poetry: A Day, by Emily Dickinson.

95
00:09:17,667 --> 00:09:18,256
See you there.

